#X msg 2500 2400 fail u_present rewire-gaveup;
#X obj 2500 2460 s err;
#X obj 200 2650 print rewire;
#X obj 1560 2160 t b b b;
#X text 1000 2760 ⛔ ONE TRAILING FORK \, AND IT BENDS PHASE 4'S RULE ON PURPOSE. That rule is one fork per load and NEVER per event \, and this is a fork on a transition. It is here because the recovery used to stop the instant the last DETECTABLE device answered \, which is not the same as the rig being whole. A device registered none -- the Volca \, which transmits nothing and can never be polled -- can be knocked off in the SAME event \, get its one attempt while it is still enumerating \, and then never be retried. Measured on the hardware 2026-08-10 \, item 275: pulling the Volca's interface also dropped the SP-404 \, the 404 came back first \, the counter reset \, and the Volca sat silently unreachable until wire.sh was run by hand. ⚠️ IT IS BOUNDED AT EXACTLY ONE PER EPISODE \, and it fires at the best moment available -- a device answering its inquiry is the signal that enumeration has FINISHED \, so a re-wire right then is the likeliest of all of them to catch whatever came back beside it., f 62;
#X text 480 2650 ⛔ THE EIGHT ATTEMPTS REACH A CONSOLE NOBODY CAN READ \, WHICH IS WHY THEY ALSO REACH err NOW. This print was added to break the silence and it only half did: the menu-launched patch runs -nogui and its stdout goes to tty1 \, which VNC will not show \, so on the actual instrument the attempts were still invisible. Measured 2026-08-10: the Volca's interface went from unsubscribed to wired on a LIVE instrument and cut-it-err.log had nothing to say about it -- no BOOT \, no device-lost \, no give-up \, and Pd's pid unchanged. Something ran wire.sh and nothing anywhere recorded it. ⚠️ IT NEEDS NO del 2000 (C-9): shell cannot emit before the fourth tick after a loss \, which is at least twelve seconds in \, and tools/deploy.sh quits its output check at about 735 ms. Same reasoning as u_init's print wire \, which is also ungated and fires at 1500 ms., f 62;
#X obj 20 2460 t b b;
#X msg 20 2720 info u_present rewire-try;
#X obj 20 2790 s err;
#X msg 1560 2300 info u_present rewire-last;
#X obj 1560 2370 s err;
#X text 20 2900 ⛔ THE TWO FORKS SAY WHICH ONE THEY ARE \, AND TELLING THEM APART IS THE POINT. rewire-try is one of the eight scheduled attempts \; rewire-last is the SINGLE trailing fork that fires when the last lost device answers \, and on the hardware those two could not be told apart -- which is the question item 275 turned on. ⚠️ info RATHER THAN warn \, and that is what stops nine alerts landing on a 21-character screen mid-set. info is logged and NEVER drawn (C-12) \, so every attempt is recorded and none of them draws over the performance. warn was tried first and oled-assert caught it immediately \, drawing over a modal the gate had asserted was undisturbed. ⚠️ THE LOG IS THE CONSUMER \, not the console., f 62;
#X obj 1900 2760 f;
#X obj 1900 2820 t f f;
#X obj 2100 2880 + 1;
#X obj 1900 2880 mod \$4;
#X obj 1900 2940 select 0;
#X msg 1900 3000 sh wire-watch.sh;
#X obj 1900 3060 shell;
#X obj 1900 3120 print wire-watch;
#X text 2400 2760 ⛔ THE HEARTBEAT THAT CATCHES A DEVICE NOTHING EVER LOST. Everything above recovers a device that WAS here and went away \, and that is gated on something being lost -- so a device registered none \, which has no clock and can never be lost \, is never recovered at all. Plug the Volca's interface into a running instrument and it enumerates in under a second and sits COMPLETELY UNSUBSCRIBED forever. Item 285 \, and ✅ seen on the rig 2026-08-10 after 1 day 21 hours up. ⚠️ THE COUNT IS TAKEN AFTER THE INCREMENT \, so the first fork lands on tick 8 and not on tick 0 -- at tick 0 it fired inside the boot window \, where u_init has just run wire.sh itself and init-assert requires EXACTLY ONE. ⚠️ THE FORK IS CHEAP AND CONDITIONAL: wire-watch.sh hashes the CLIENT NAMES and runs wire.sh only when they change -- measured 50 ms against wire.sh's 247 ms \, and the hash is byte-identical across a re-wire so the watcher cannot see its own work and retrigger. That is what keeps this a fork on a real EVENT rather than a fork on a timer \, which is the distinction Phase 4's rule is actually about., f 62;
#X obj 1000 2500 t b b;
#X msg 1000 2620 info u_present rewire-phone;
#X obj 1000 2680 s err;
#X text 2500 1520 ⛔ A THIRD CAUSE OF A FORK \, AND IT IS THE ONLY ONE A PERSON ASKS FOR. The eight scheduled attempts and the one trailing fork are both automatic and both BOUNDED -- and once the bound is spent the rig stays wired to nothing until the patch is reloaded \, which restarts everything you were doing. re-wire on this bus is the phone's button \, and it reaches the SAME sh wire.sh message box the other two do \, so there is still exactly one owner of the recovery and one place it can happen. ⚠️ IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT A RESET OF THE COUNTER: one fork per press \, paced by a finger \, which is the only rate limit an on-demand recovery can honestly have. ⛔ AND IT NEEDS ITS OWN NAME ON err FOR THE REASON THE OTHER TWO DO -- all three converge on one message box \, so a report tapped below the junction could not tell them apart \, and telling them apart is the whole question when reading a log afterwards. The trigger fires the FORK from its right outlet and the report from its left \, exactly as the scheduled one does., f 62;
